WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.840
Thank you for taking a break from Starfield
to watch this video.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.849
Indiana Jones and the Dial of Destiny begins
in a flashback at the end of WW2 where Indy

00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:13.380
is hunting down an artifact that the nazis
have and he’s also been captured.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.060
From here, the movie starts pretty quickly
in terms of thrusting us into a ridiculous

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:22.060
scene in which Indiana Jones miraculously
survives being hanged, while also having a

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:25.520
bomb go off in the tower that causes him to
fling all over the place.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.029
So before going into all of that craziness,
why are the Nazis even bothering about going

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:32.820
through all this trouble in killing Indiana
Jones.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:37.670
They’re literally staring at him with guns,
but they decide to go all the way up to the

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.190
top of this tower and then set up a rope to
hang their prisoner.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:45.020
Why not just take him out back and execute
him with bullets?

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.780
Especially given that the entire compound
is evacuating with some haste.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.239
But this is obviously done so that Indiana
has some chance to escape.

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:56.620
And the means in which he escapes is just
so baffling that the movie is already off

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.430
to a rough start.

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.930
Surely this won’t be the only extreme convenience
that happens.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:05.980
After getting out of the tower Indiana Jones
steals a car and knocks out the driver with

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.259
his iconic one punch KO.

00:01:08.259 --> 00:01:10.909
This is a movie stereotype, but I’ll accept
it.

00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:15.150
The more annoying aspect to me is that Indy
is literally doing this in the middle of a

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.189
courtyard in full view of dozens of people.

00:01:18.189 --> 00:01:21.799
Then some officers get into the car and don’t
even question the fact about why there’s

00:01:21.799 --> 00:01:24.900
a passed out soldier right next to the driver
seat.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.200
Or there even being a motorcycle right behind
the car he’s hijacking that definitely would

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.580
have saw someone get tossed out of the vehicle.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:35.240
So were 2 for 2 already and it only gets worse
from here.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.200
Indy eventually goes off course to chase after
the Train in which the artifact he’s after

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.200
is on.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.979
So he has to take out some bikers and ends
up hijacking one of them as they dive into

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:44.979
the forest.

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:50.060
Now to say that Indy is extremely lucky in
this scene would be an understatement because

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.690
they somehow don’t hit a single tree despite
not looking at where they’re going.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:59.259
He literally has the luck of Domino from Deadpool
2 in this movie so far.

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:02.920
Not to mention that he’s actively fighting
someone over control of the bike.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.670
So (by sheer dumb luck - Harry Potter 1) Indiana
Jones gets onto the train.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:11.160
And does all the devoid of logic nonsense
finally stop here?

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.910
Of course it doesn’t.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:17.110
Indy saves his friend Baz and while they’re
running away from the Nazis down the train,

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.300
some planes bomb the anti-air gun.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:24.030
Now everything up until this point was pretty
lucky, but to me this takes the cake as being

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.170
the luckiest.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:30.160
So the anti-air gun doesn’t actually get
fully destroyed and perfectly spins around

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.349
right to the exact angle to where it shoots
at the Nazis pursuing Indy.

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:38.909
And the train just so happens to be going
through a turn so that the AA gun is firing

00:02:38.909 --> 00:02:41.270
at the enemy and not the heroes.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:45.800
This so conveniently ends up essentially wiping
out all of the Nazis chasing them and if the

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:49.769
writers writing this moment think this was
good by any means then they’re delusional.

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:55.379
We want to see our heroes overcome major conflict
without the will of god solving it for them.

00:02:55.379 --> 00:02:59.550
So since most of the Nazis on the train are
dead now, it allows the more prominent villain

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.100
to have a fight with Indy.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.811
However, what immediately doesn’t make any
sense is why the villain doesn’t have a

00:03:04.811 --> 00:03:07.550
gun that he simply shoots Indy with.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:12.129
He literally had one 5 minutes prior while
questioning Baz and for some reason he opts

00:03:12.129 --> 00:03:14.400
to use the artifact as a weapon.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.490
What a nice guy giving his opponent a better
advantage.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:22.470
But wait, he actually does have his gun and
he doesn’t use it and Indiana Jones steals

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:23.790
it while they are wrestling.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.010
It gets knocked away and now Baz has the gun.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:31.110
Instead of chasing after the gun, the villain
simply allows himself to be shot by Baz with

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.330
virtually no protest.

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:36.280
You’d think someone pointing a gun at you
would be more of an imminent threat than a

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:37.909
guy with a whip, but whatever.

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:43.180
Then Dr. Voller, whose the main villain scientist,
feels left out so he also decides to join

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.250
them on the roof since he’s after the Antikythera.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:46.250
Which up until this point is a mystery as
to what it does.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:50.040
At least he isn’t dumb enough to not initiate
their confrontation with a gun.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:54.170
Indy hands it over to him and then while on
the side of the train, Dr. Voller gets bonked

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:56.209
on the head by a metal pipe.

00:03:56.209 --> 00:03:58.720
Now what do you think happens from this point?

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:04.400
Does Voller A: flat out die on the spot from
pure blunt force trauma, B: does he become

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.739
a vegetable from his brain being so destroyed,
or C: does he simply get a small scar on his

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:12.220
forehead with no clear mental impairments.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:17.940
Hey you picked the depressingly obvious answer
that is C. The writers basically make Dr.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:21.910
Voller an impenetrable superhero for being
able to survive this.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:26.500
The train was moving at probably about 40ish
miles per hour and he got struck in the head

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:28.160
by a metal pipe.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:32.160
If I were to just fall down on the ground
and slam my head onto the concrete I’d probably

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.540
have to go to the hospital, let alone getting
slammed in the head by a pipe at high speeds.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.009
The man should have been decapitated at that
force and speed.

00:04:41.009 --> 00:04:44.150
If anyone has seen Hereditary then you know
what’s up.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:48.610
So yeah, the prologue for this movie feels
like I’m watching the adventures of Tintin

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:53.410
instead of an Indiana Jones movie from how
crazy and absurd all the action setpieces

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:55.070
have been so far.

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:59.630
Going to present day, Baz’s daughter Helena
finds and confronts Indy and they talk about

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:01.010
the Antikythera.

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:04.630
It turns out that she’s a not an actual
good person and is someone who sells prized

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:07.010
artifacts on the black market.

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:09.770
Which is what she intends to do with the Antikythera.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:14.389
It also turns out that Voller is alive and
is pursuing Helena, along with US government

00:05:14.389 --> 00:05:16.070
agents cooperating with him.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:18.220
Given that he helped the US land on the moon.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:24.080
Voller also has his own goons who are much
more loyal to him, but are also very sloppy.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:27.910
Because while in pursuit of Indy and Helena,
they murder two upstanding civilians in cold

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:28.910
blood.

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:32.639
Which right away is pretty insane that the
government agents wouldn’t have immediately

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:35.610
cut all ties and have arrested them on the
spot.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:40.160
I’m pretty sure some random chick selling
stolen goods isn’t as important as killing

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.880
two civilians, but I don’t know what the
writers are smoking.

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:49.100
So a chase initiates and Helena screws over
Indy by leaving him behind by locking a gate.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:53.360
This seems unnecessarily cruel given that
he’s her godfather and she’s essentially

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.480
handing him over to the wolves.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:59.210
Like he’s on her side and I don’t know
why she needed to betray him here.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:03.300
I’ll go more into this subject later on
in how it impacts her character arc, and her

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.590
relationship with Indy throughout the movie.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:09.900
But because of this, Indy gets captured and
has to escape the bad guys.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:13.979
Which I’m not entirely sure why they even
need him since Voller already knows where

00:06:13.979 --> 00:06:16.419
the auction for the Dial is going to be held.

00:06:16.419 --> 00:06:21.040
They’re literally just kidnapping this teacher
for no apparent reason, other than he briefly

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:22.610
interacted with Helena.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:26.319
For all they know she just stole that relic
from him given that she visually betrayed

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:27.319
him.

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:30.970
But yeah, he ends up getting away by physically
besting his opponents.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:33.110
Now let that sink in for a second.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:38.449
Harrison Ford is 80 years old and he has enough
strength to overcome these muscular brutes

00:06:38.449 --> 00:06:39.979
in their prime?

00:06:39.979 --> 00:06:42.449
Along with him being able to run away from
them.

00:06:42.449 --> 00:06:46.130
Like they should easily be able to catch up
but I guess they can’t?

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:51.000
So Indy hijacks a horse from a cop that disappears
and a chase initiates.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.690
Indy ends up conveniently escaping in the
subways in which he luckily doesn’t get

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:59.160
decimated by the trains and has perfect timing
when it comes to getting into one.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:03.630
Still not sure why the bad guys want him so
badly, but heyyyy, we wouldn’t have an action

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.740
scene so don’t question that.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:10.379
I feel like this would inherently be a better
action scene if it was done via Helena escaping

00:07:10.379 --> 00:07:11.920
these bad guys.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:14.760
Given that she’s much younger and would
be more believable.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:17.910
Instead, they make it to where she quickly
gets away.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:22.310
Moving on, Indiana Jones is now wanted for
murder since the police somehow came to the

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:25.569
conclusion that Indy was probably the one
to kill his colleagues.

00:07:25.569 --> 00:07:28.750
I guess he really didn’t like that retirement
gift.

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:33.660
So now Indy is a wanted man and they are publicly
showing his face on the news.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:37.380
So since he’s in a pinch, he calls his friend
Sallah to taxi him to the airport.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:42.380
I’m not really sure why that was necessary
and why he didn’t just call a normal cab.

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:45.810
This character’s return because of this
doesn’t really have much of a point and

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:49.520
feels more like a lazy attempt to garner nostalgia
from the audience.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.190
If you’d think it’s because Indy’s popular
now from his face being all over the news

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:57.879
and needs to be more incognito, then that
would make sense, but that’s immediately

00:07:57.879 --> 00:08:01.120
contradicted by going through an airport without
being recognized.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:06.490
I know back in the day airport security was
light, but surely there are probably some

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:10.599
police present, especially since there’s
a murderer on the loose.

00:08:10.599 --> 00:08:14.180
I feel like that would be an absolute given
to upgrade security in the area.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:15.180
But nope.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:19.150
Indiana Jones simply just gets on his plane
and goes to Morroco in which the auction for

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:21.570
the Antikythera is being held.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:26.099
Also while on the plane, there’s this whole
flashback of Indiana Jones taking the Antikythera

00:08:26.099 --> 00:08:28.970
from Baz and has a brief encounter with Helena.

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:33.750
However, this exact moment was discussed between
Indy and Helena so it feels completely unnecessary

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.260
to include this.

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:40.100
This also especially feels worse given that
the movie is almost three hours long so having

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:43.900
to sit through what should have been a deleted
scene is rough.

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:48.370
Moving onto the next action setpiece, Indy
shows up to the auction unannounced and crashes

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:49.370
the party.

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:53.800
A fight for the dial inevitably ensues and
at some point, the dial flies into Indy’s

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:54.800
hands.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:59.220
When it does there’s this brief moment in
which Indy punches a bad guy and he goes flying.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:03.410
Which I find hilarious that these GUNS are
packing that much power.

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:07.550
Now this particular shot of Indy with his
whip facing an onslaught of guns is inherently

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:08.550
really silly.

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:12.329
I remember this being one of the main shots
in the trailer, but I was always like how

00:09:12.329 --> 00:09:15.740
the hell would Indiana realistically get out
of that situation.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:20.550
And realistically he can’t because the bad
guys just comedically shoot at nothing while

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:24.110
he ducks under the table and they don’t
bother shooting under it.

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:28.730
After this, Teddy steals the dial and then
runs right into Voller who steals it in return.

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:29.800
How convenient.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:33.640
Voller then takes the dial into a car with
his goons and they drive off.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:37.640
Indy and Helena would pursue them, but they
are stopped by a mob boss that Helena married

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.690
for bail money and obviously used.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:44.510
He’s very upset with her and is essentially
threatening her, and yet, Indy comes in like

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:52.950
a child complaining to his teacher that Helena
stole his candy.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:54.680
Like why would he care?

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:58.800
You are barging into this conversation and
are disrupting a mob boss.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.550
In any real-world scenario, the mob boss would
simply kill the person disrespecting him like

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:07.070
that, but this comes across as such a childish
script.

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:11.540
So eventually Helena upsets the mob guy and
they are now being chased by them.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:15.490
During this whole chase scene, I find it really
admirable that Harrison Ford is running around

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:20.510
given his age, but at no point do I ever buy
the fact that he’s able to outrun anyone

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:22.500
or should be able to keep up with Helena.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:24.800
He’s basically hobbling around.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:29.640
They eventually steal a vehicle and get chased
around a bit and then through divine intervention

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:34.110
from the script writer gods, they randomly
run into Dr. Voller.

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:38.670
Now this may not seem like that big of a deal
from watching this video, however, so much

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:43.490
time has passed since he drove off that there
is no possible way they were able to catch

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:46.000
up to him, or let alone find him.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.260
So this is obviously a crazy coincidence and
is a contrived way for the heroes to fight

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:51.260
the bad guys.

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:56.250
Not only does this happen once, it actually
happens twice because they go off on another

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:59.040
side tangent of getting chased by the mobsters.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.800
This entire scene is just so nauseating in
terms of where all these characters are in

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.430
relation to each other and it’s as if they
are teleporting around wherever the writers

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:10.190
need them to be without following any logic.

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:15.700
Also during this whole chase let me show you
this moment.

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:19.240
Pause.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:23.250
Now do you think the vehicle they are in tumbles
down this stairwell and they all die, or do

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:26.529
they conveniently survive without any injuries?

00:11:26.529 --> 00:11:30.700
The fact that you instinctively picked the
second option is very worrying for the writing

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:32.820
and how they present this ridiculous action.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:37.300
It’s also pretty crazy that they are able
to come to full stop after rocketing down

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:40.190
this stairwell and don’t explode into the
wall at the bottom.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:44.810
What’s even more ridiculous is that the
vehicle is fine and is still able to drive

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:46.350
after this.

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:51.130
But yeah, from here it’s just a circus of
devoid of logic antics that again makes this

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:55.700
movie feel like Adventures of Tintin and any
grounded and realistic action from the first

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:57.889
few movies are nonexistent.

00:11:57.889 --> 00:12:01.420
So the bad guys get away and overthrow their
US associates.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:05.639
Apparently, the final blow for the US agents
was getting arrested in a foreign country

00:12:05.639 --> 00:12:06.971
and not killing US civilians.

00:12:06.971 --> 00:12:07.971
Also, this quote doesn’t seem to make sense.

00:12:07.971 --> 00:12:08.971
(US agent says 3 civilians were killed) If
I remember correctly they only killed two

00:12:08.971 --> 00:12:09.971
people, but maybe they killed another in a
deleted scene or something.

00:12:09.971 --> 00:12:13.680
Going over to Indiana and Helena they have
some riveting discussions like this.

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:19.930
It’s kind of annoying that the writers are
making their interactions this juvenile and

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:23.810
so far we are basically half way through the
movie and they are insufferable.

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:28.760
They finally decide to work together after
Indy practically begs to tag along and they

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:31.160
meet up with Antonio Banderas.

00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:35.680
Pretty convenient that he knows a diver in
the exact area they need to dive in, but whatever,

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:38.639
between everything that’s happened that’s
the one thing I’ll buy.

00:12:38.639 --> 00:12:43.250
While on the boat, Indy eventually tells Helena
that his son who was played by Shia LaBeouf

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:44.480
died in the military.

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:49.010
At the very least the writers acknowledge
that the character existed, but it still stings

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:52.430
that they nonchalantly kill his character
offscreen.

00:12:52.430 --> 00:12:56.300
Also this scene in terms of dialogue lacks
subtlety since Indy lays out everything to

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:59.440
a character he isn’t even getting along
with.

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.699
The characters dive down and get the next
piece of the puzzle that they are chasing

00:13:02.699 --> 00:13:06.060
after, and Voller shows up and hijacks the
ship.

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:09.930
They arrive while they are diving which is
insane timing given that they were only going

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:12.470
to be down there for 3 minutes.

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:16.520
So that’s a contrived way to give control
over to the villains since our heroes weren’t

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:17.520
present.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:21.700
They also cut one of the lines to oxygen for
one of the divers and luckily it wasn’t

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:23.010
an important character.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:27.800
I also don’t understand why this random
NPC didn’t just activate his life vest and

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:30.480
simply rise to the surface after losing air.

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:35.110
Instead, he chokes to death and falls into
the abyss and dies within seconds.

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:39.010
Surely he would have been able to hold his
breath a little longer since these are divers,

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:46.610
but heyyyy, don’t think about that.

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:47.610
Get it!??

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:48.920
Because of time travel.

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:52.890
So Voller steals the graphikos tablet which
will point them to the final piece of the

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:56.130
Antikythera and asks Indy to decipher it.

00:13:56.130 --> 00:14:00.540
Indy in this moment makes the decision to
not give in and by doing so I guess he’s

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:03.100
alright with having all of his friends be
murdered.

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:07.490
Because of this, Antonio Banderas is deleted
out of the movie and it just feels so odd

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:11.490
to have him be in the movie if he’s only
going to be given like 5 lines.

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:15.920
In response to this, Indy makes a cute attempt
to try and tackle this hulk of a human and

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:17.200
obviously fails.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:20.490
What were you expecting you’re 80 years
old man!

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:23.980
Since Indy doesn’t want to decipher the
tablet and we’ve run out of background characters

00:14:23.980 --> 00:14:28.310
to kill, Helena steps up to the plate and
asks for compensation.

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:31.750
Not sure why she waited for someone to be
killed and at this point I assume she’s

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:33.029
a flat out sociopath.

00:14:33.029 --> 00:14:37.800
Also, Voller has complete authority in this
situation and is threatening them, so him

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:41.620
agreeing to hand over 100 grand to Helena
is pretty surprising.

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:46.000
All he has to do is threaten to kill her kid
companion and she’ll immediately cave.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:49.790
While deciphering the tablet she shows Indy
that she has a stick of dynamite that was

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:53.250
glaringly set up earlier, even though it didn’t
show her grab any.

00:14:53.250 --> 00:14:56.980
The shot directly after this in which she
turns around and is greeted to the hulk looming

00:14:56.980 --> 00:14:58.760
over her is pretty funny though.

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:02.680
This guy is literally staring down at her
and didn’t even notice her pull something

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:04.889
out of her pocket to show Indy.

00:15:04.889 --> 00:15:08.130
Surely he had line of sight, but I guess he’s
just zoned out.

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:11.601
Even after this, the blocking of this scene
assumes that you forget these two goons in

00:15:11.601 --> 00:15:13.500
the corner are even there.

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:17.500
Because Helena is lighting the stick of dynamite
within full view of them.

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:21.420
Even when she does light it, they aren’t
even active within the scene and don’t immediately

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:22.899
capture Indy and Helena.

00:15:22.899 --> 00:15:26.970
They’re just standing in the corner as if
the director put them in time out.

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:31.279
So since they evaporated from the scene, the
director created a contrived sense of believability

00:15:31.279 --> 00:15:32.389
for them to escape.

00:15:32.389 --> 00:15:36.540
I also love how Indy when getting into the
boat doesn’t even bother to take cover when

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:37.620
getting shot at.

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:41.470
His plot armor is so thick that bullets don’t
scare him anymore.

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:46.410
From here, Indy sets the wooden boat on fire
like a mad lad and reveals a golden dial underneath

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:47.639
the tablet.

00:15:47.639 --> 00:15:51.980
The fact that he then picks it up immediately
is pretty insane given how hot it should be.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:56.650
Now we're in Sicily since this is where the
next half of the Antikythera is located.

00:15:56.650 --> 00:16:00.880
While here, Teddy has an integral scene of
eating ice cream and by complete and utter

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:04.510
fate he stumbles across the bad guys chilling
on the docks.

00:16:04.510 --> 00:16:09.340
I find it funny how one of the goons menacingly
walks out of the shadows like Batman as if

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:12.029
he’s been waiting for Teddy to fall into
his trap.

00:16:12.029 --> 00:16:16.950
This scene gives off the same energy as Euron
Greyjoy swimming ashore right when Jaime was

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:17.950
passing through.

00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:21.540
Then since Teddy has gone missing, Indy picks
a random place to check on his whereabouts

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:24.500
and heyyyy, he just so happens to witness
Teddy get kidnapped.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:28.889
It’s only as if this is a big city and Teddy
could literally be anywhere.

00:16:28.889 --> 00:16:33.170
Yet another contrivance to give Indy the confirmation
that Teddy has been kidnapped.

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:37.620
So now Indy and Helena need to steal a car
which like the opening is extremely easy since

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:40.810
the awareness of these background actors is
nonexistent.

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:45.139
Indy simply walks up to the car, opens it,
and drives off; and by the time he starts

00:16:45.139 --> 00:16:47.620
the engine, that’s when these people notice.

00:16:47.620 --> 00:16:50.139
Not when you know, they slam the door shut.

00:16:50.139 --> 00:16:54.730
Out of all the ways to steal a vehicle I don’t
know why it needs to be this devoid of logic.

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:58.579
While on the way there Indy says this “They’re
not gonna hurt him.

00:16:58.579 --> 00:17:00.269
They’re gonna use him.”

00:17:00.269 --> 00:17:01.510
How would you even know!?

00:17:01.510 --> 00:17:06.319
They’re goddamn Nazis and have proven in
the past to be extremely ruthless and they’ve

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:08.110
already killed a bunch of people.

00:17:08.110 --> 00:17:10.390
But they draw the line at kids?

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:14.860
Arriving at the tomb of Archimedes, Indy and
Helena somehow get there before Voller despite

00:17:14.860 --> 00:17:16.280
them being behind.

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:20.480
By this point, since there have been so many
dumb aspects bombarding the viewer, they are

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:24.010
probably so numb to it that they didn’t
even question this moment.

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:27.780
From here, this is really the only solid portion
of the movie I didn’t hate since it includes

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:29.680
some fun tomb raiding.

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:33.880
Although it still has some hard to believe
aspects in the script like this area barely

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:37.950
being explored despite it being a popular
location, this light revealing the way to

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:42.830
go while the sun clearly isn’t visible anymore,
Teddy escaping the bad guys by forcing him

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:48.160
and his captor into the river that has a convenient
kid sized hole for him to escape, and Indy

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:50.990
being able to rock climb given his stellar
physical condition.

00:17:50.990 --> 00:17:58.169
I do give the movie some bonus points for
this funny rant that Indy goes on though.

00:17:58.169 --> 00:18:04.809
Inevitably, the bad guys catch up to Indy
and Helena and Teddy also found his way into

00:18:04.809 --> 00:18:07.280
the tomb even though he was swept away in
a river.

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:10.790
I’m kind of just quickly touching on these
really dumb aspects when in reality these

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:12.200
are major deals.

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:16.470
They’re becoming so numerous to the point
that I’m becoming desensitized to the stupidity

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:17.900
in this movie.

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:21.350
So Teddy is up on this big slab of rock and
Helena spots him.

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:26.080
While the bad guys behind her who have a better
view of the whole room somehow don’t.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:29.750
Also like the boat scene, these background
bad guys won’t do anything when the action

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:34.620
pops off because if they were actually active
they’d easily be able to kill the good guys

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:36.500
since they’re right behind them.

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:41.220
So after some brief action, Indy tells Helena
and Teddy to get out of there and Indy is

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:42.440
now captured.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:47.150
For whatever baffling reason, the nazis don’t
quickly pursue after Helena and Teddy and

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:48.260
capture them as well.

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:51.100
But if that were the case, the movie would
instantly end.

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:55.850
Also, Indy’s plot armor is starting to get
unbearably thick since Voller suddenly wants

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:57.100
to keep Indy alive.

00:18:57.100 --> 00:19:01.520
I don’t why this is the case since they
have no relationship or dynamic given how

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:03.620
fast their past encounters have been.

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:07.770
They’ve basically have had barely any back
and forth dialogue throughout the movie and

00:19:07.770 --> 00:19:11.630
the writers expect us to buy the fact that
Voller wants to keep him around to witness

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:12.630
his victory?

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:14.380
Like why would he even care?

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:18.740
So now through yet another contrivance, Indy
gets a front row seat to watch the climax

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:19.740
of the movie.

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:23.870
Helena and Teddy catch up to the Nazis as
they are boarding their plane and Helena manages

00:19:23.870 --> 00:19:26.880
to narrowly climb through the landing gear
before it takes off.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:31.390
For Teddy, he hijacks a plane that’s conveniently
ready to take off and is able to get the plane

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:32.610
into the sky.

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:37.600
This may seem wild, but at the bare minimum,
the movie set up an infatuation he has with

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:41.640
planes so this is funnily one of the least
dumb aspects so far.

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:46.030
However, the main question is going to be
how this kid will land the plane successfully.

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:52.600
Will he A: Somehow land the plane like a pro,
B: crash the plane and walk away with no injuries,

00:19:52.600 --> 00:19:57.351
or C: there’s a pilot randomly sleeping
behind him who lands the plane for him.

00:19:57.351 --> 00:19:59.080
And the answer is C!

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:03.620
What a miracle that this pilot is here or
the story physically won’t be able to operate.

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:05.850
I feel like that’s what this movie has been
so far.

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:10.050
It’s the writers connecting all of these
idiotic aspects with duck tape and hoping

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:12.250
it functions in any way.

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:16.289
Moving on to the fun bit, we have the actual
time travel portion of the movie.

00:20:16.289 --> 00:20:20.070
Now even after watching the movie twice I
still don’t understand how this dial is

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:23.100
able to get someone to a precise place and
time in the past.

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:27.630
But yeah, between everything in the movie,
I’ll just buy that time travel exists and

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:32.530
instead of going back to Germany, they are
teleported to the Siege of Syracuse in 214

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:33.540
BC.

00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:37.559
This is where the movie finally gains a small
ounce of fun as everyone freaks out about

00:20:37.559 --> 00:20:39.130
being sent to the past.

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:43.270
The most hilarious moment during this scene
was when one of the Nazis opens fire on the

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:44.270
Romans.

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:48.200
When this happened I bursted out laughing
in the theater from how ludicrously ridiculous

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:49.200
it was.

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:53.530
I just don’t know what he was trying to
achieve here, that’s not going to do anything.

00:20:53.530 --> 00:20:57.860
So eventually when flying around they slowly
get brought down by numerous scorpion bolts

00:20:57.860 --> 00:21:01.659
and don’t pay any attention to the fact
that the plane Teddy is on hasn’t been hit.

00:21:01.659 --> 00:21:06.200
It may be smaller, but given that they are
getting spammed by scorpion shots, it’s

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:08.690
pretty lucky that they’re unscathed.

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:12.940
Just before the plane crashes, Indy and Helena
jump out with the only parachute and share

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:15.040
it together as they float down to the ground.

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:19.929
They’re greeted by Archimedes who brings
them back the Antikythera that he conveniently

00:21:19.929 --> 00:21:21.450
found from the crash site.

00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:25.730
So out of all the devoid of logic aspects,
the movie surprisingly makes sense from a

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:29.790
time travel perspective and doesn’t make
a glaring paradox.

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:32.790
Although partially but whatever.

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:37.530
During this scene, Indy wants to stay behind
since he’s still shot and is slowly dying.

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:41.600
Pretty irresponsible of him honestly in terms
of how he’ll change the future, but this

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:46.010
is actually a pretty emotional scene and Harrison
Ford did an excellent job here.

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:49.990
He delivers a lot of sincerity and you can
really feel the amount of care and love he

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:51.360
has for the character.

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:54.980
It does feel a bit weird in the moment since
you are like “are they actually going to

00:21:54.980 --> 00:21:59.860
kill another beloved character like Han Solo”
but luckily they don’t commit to that and

00:21:59.860 --> 00:22:02.059
Helena knocks his ass out.

00:22:02.059 --> 00:22:07.110
On one hand I like this reversal in having
Indy get one punched KO’d, but on the other

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:11.920
hand, the man is 80 years old and this would
probably be a death sentence for the guy given

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:16.200
that getting a concussion at that age probably
isn’t the best.

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:21.080
So since he gets knocked out we hard cut back
to Indy’s apartment and everything is resolved.

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:25.100
That feels a little cheap, but since the movie
is already creeping up on the mid 2 hour mark

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:27.539
I’ll take that as the director doing us
a solid.

00:22:27.539 --> 00:22:31.300
Now taking a look at Helena’s character,
I personally think that her arc here in that

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:36.400
she cares deeply about Indy and desperately
wants to save his life is unearned.

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:40.740
Especially from an emotional standpoint because
she’s left him to die on multiple occasions

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:43.580
and they only had a few brief scenes of bonding.

00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:47.800
So her drastically changing and suddenly not
caring about money anymore doesn’t feel

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:49.620
like it was properly built to.

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:54.020
I like this arc in theory, but the writers
butchered her character by making her so cold

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:56.230
and heartless for half the movie.

00:22:56.230 --> 00:23:01.600
So yeah, now Indy teleported back to his apartment
and we get a happily ever after with his wife

00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:02.600
returning.

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:06.960
Just forget about the fact that he’s still
a wanted man for allegedly murdering his colleagues.

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:09.850
And that’s Indiana Jones and the Dial of
Destiny.

00:23:09.850 --> 00:23:14.250
It’s pretty sad that this movie ended up
having such a dumb and juvenile script and

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:18.169
I was wishing for something that was still
fun while not disregarding logic in every

00:23:18.169 --> 00:23:19.460
second of the film.

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:23.429
Since the first few Indiana Jones movies were
grounded in logic.

00:23:23.429 --> 00:23:27.549
Besides Crystall of Skull, that movie also
deserves to be stored away and be forgotten

00:23:27.549 --> 00:23:28.549
about.

00:23:28.549 --> 00:23:31.070
Thank you for watching and let me know in
the comments what you thought about The Dial

00:23:31.070 --> 00:23:32.090
of Destiny.

00:23:32.090 --> 00:23:35.009
And follow me on Letterboxd for more reviews
on movies.

